---
import CommandBlock from '../components/CommandBlock.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import ManualLinuxSetup from '../components/download/ManualLinuxSetup.astro';
import PlatformChooser from '../components/download/PlatformChooser.astro';
import PluginDownload from '../components/download/PluginDownload.astro';
import {
  ANDROID_ASSET,
  ANDROID_URL,
  BREW_COMMAND,
  CHOCO_COMMAND,
  INSTALL_SCRIPT_COMMAND,
  MACOS_ASSET,
  MACOS_URL,
  MOBILE_RELEASES_URL,
  RELEASES_URL,
  SCOOP_COMMAND,
  WINDOWS_ASSET,
  WINDOWS_URL,
} from '../data/install-channels';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

// Wording mandated verbatim by the SignPath Foundation code of conduct. It is
// kept as one contiguous string, with the links in a separate sentence, so a
// reviewer searching the rendered page finds it intact.
const signingAttribution = 'Free code signing provided by SignPath.io, certificate by SignPath Foundation';

const primaryLink =
  'inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-lg bg-accent px-3.5 py-1.5 text-body-md font-medium text-on-accent transition-opacity duration-fast hover:opacity-90';
const secondaryLink =
  'text-body-md text-foreground-faint underline underline-offset-4 transition-colors duration-fast hover:text-foreground';
const inlineLink = 'text-foreground underline underline-offset-4 transition-opacity duration-fast hover:opacity-80';
const sectionTitle = 'mb-3 scroll-mt-20 text-headline-sm text-foreground';
const body = 'mb-4 text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-muted';
const note = 'mb-4 text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-faint';
---

<Layout
  title="Download Alera - Install On macOS, Windows, Linux, And Android"
  description="Download Alera for macOS, Windows, and Android, or install it on Linux from the signed package repository."
  canonicalPath="/download"
>
  <Navbar />
  <main class="px-6 pt-24 pb-20">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <h1 class="mb-4 text-headline-lg font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground md:text-[32px]">Download Alera</h1>
      <p class="mb-10 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-foreground-muted">
        The desktop workbench runs on macOS, Windows, and Linux. The companion mobile app runs on Android.
      </p>

      <PlatformChooser />

      <PluginDownload />

      <h2 id="macos" class={sectionTitle}>macOS</h2>
      <p class={body}>Install with Homebrew. Requires Apple Silicon and macOS 14 or newer.</p>
      <div class="mb-4"><CommandBlock command={BREW_COMMAND} /></div>
      <p class={note}>
        The cask clears the quarantine attribute after installing, because the macOS build is not notarized yet. Or
        unpack <span class="break-all font-mono">{MACOS_ASSET}</span> and move Alera.app to your Applications folder.
      </p>
      <div class="mb-12 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href={MACOS_URL} class={primaryLink}>Download For macOS</a>
        <a href={RELEASES_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={secondaryLink}>All Releases</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="windows" class={sectionTitle}>Windows</h2>
      <p class={body}>Install with Scoop, which needs no administrator rights.</p>
      <div class="mb-4"><CommandBlock command={SCOOP_COMMAND} /></div>
      <p class={body}>Or with Chocolatey, from an elevated prompt.</p>
      <div class="mb-4"><CommandBlock command={CHOCO_COMMAND} /></div>
      <p class={note}>
        Requires 64-bit Windows. Or unpack <span class="break-all font-mono">{WINDOWS_ASSET}</span> anywhere and run
        Alera.exe.
      </p>
      <div class="mb-12 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href={WINDOWS_URL} class={primaryLink}>Download For Windows</a>
        <a href={RELEASES_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={secondaryLink}>All Releases</a>
      </div>

      <h2 id="linux" class={sectionTitle}>Linux</h2>
      <p class={body}>
        Alera installs from a signed package repository so system dependencies resolve through apt or dnf. The same
        command installs and updates.
      </p>
      <div class="mb-4"><CommandBlock command={INSTALL_SCRIPT_COMMAND} /></div>
      <p class="mb-12 text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-faint">
        Requires x86_64 and Ubuntu 24.04 or newer, Debian 13 or newer, or Fedora. On RHEL, Rocky Linux, and AlmaLinux,
        enable EPEL first, which is where <span class="font-mono">libayatana-appindicator-gtk3</span> comes from.
        openSUSE is not supported yet: the published RPM declares Fedora package names that openSUSE provides under
        different names.
      </p>

      <h2 id="android" class={sectionTitle}>Android</h2>
      <p class={body}>
        The companion app pairs with the runtime on your desktop over your own network, or through the optional
        encrypted relay; it is not a standalone editor. Mobile ships on its own version sequence, so its releases carry a
        <span class="font-mono">-mobile</span> tag suffix. This is the 64-bit Android APK
        (<span class="break-all font-mono">{ANDROID_ASSET}</span>) for phones such as a Pixel 7a.
      </p>
      <div class="mb-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <a href={ANDROID_URL} class={primaryLink}>Download The APK</a>
        <a href={MOBILE_RELEASES_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={secondaryLink}>
          All Mobile Releases
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="mb-12 text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-faint">
        Sideloading an APK means Android asks you to allow installs from your browser or file manager. There is no Play
        Store listing yet. Then <a href="/docs/mobile" class={inlineLink}>pair the phone</a> with your desktop.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ios" class={sectionTitle}>iOS</h2>
      <div class="mb-14 rounded-lg border border-border bg-surface-variant p-5">
        <span class="mb-3 inline-flex items-center rounded-pill border border-border px-2.5 py-1 text-label-md text-foreground-faint">
          Coming Soon
        </span>
        <p class="text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-muted">
          There is no iOS build to install yet. The companion app targets Android today; iOS is planned and has no date.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ManualLinuxSetup />

      <h2 id="code-signing-policy" class={sectionTitle}>Code Signing Policy</h2>
      <p class="mb-4 text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground">{signingAttribution}.</p>
      <p class={body}>
        <a href="https://about.signpath.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={inlineLink}>SignPath.io</a>
        runs the signing service, and the
        <a href="https://signpath.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={inlineLink}>SignPath Foundation</a>
        issues the certificate to open source projects at no cost.
      </p>
      <p class={body}>
        Alera is maintained by one person, who fills every role:
        <a href="https://leynier.dev" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class={inlineLink}>Leynier Gutiérrez González</a>
        is the sole committer, reviewer, and approver of signing requests.
      </p>
      <p class={body}>
        Data handling is described in the <a href="/privacy" class={inlineLink}>Privacy Policy</a>. Alera is
        local-first: your projects, terminals, and settings stay on your machine.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body-md leading-relaxed text-foreground-faint">
        Current status, so you know what to expect from your system: Linux packages are distributed through a
        repository whose metadata is signed with the key above. Windows and macOS builds are
        <strong class="text-foreground-muted">not signed yet</strong>, so Windows SmartScreen reports an unknown
        publisher and macOS Gatekeeper asks you to allow the app explicitly. Windows signing through SignPath begins
        once the certificate is issued.
      </p>
    </div>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
